    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Three minor, three major, one diminished, and the mix of them is the surprise: "
        },
        {
          "text": "Am",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "Dm",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "text": "Em",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " are minor; "
        },
        {
          "text": "C",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "F",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "text": "G",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " are major; "
        },
        {
          "text": "Bdim",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is neither. A minor key is not made of minor chords."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "tone": "info",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Three chords in the key are minor, three are major, one is diminished. A minor key is not made of minor chords."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "One naming convention, defined once and used for the rest of this chapter: lowercase for a minor chord ("
        },
        {
          "text": "i",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "iv",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "v",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "), uppercase for a major one ("
        },
        {
          "text": "III",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "VI",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "VII",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "), and "
        },
        {
          "text": "°",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " for diminished ("
        },
        {
          "text": "ii°",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "). These are Roman numerals, and this chapter uses them the whole way through — the numeral tells you the degree and its case tells you the quality, so you never have to say both."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Now harmonise C major the same way — stack thirds on "
        },
        {
          "text": "C D E F G A B",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — and you get "
        },
        {
          "text": "C Dm Em F G Am Bdim",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ": the identical seven chords, started from the sixth. "
        },
        {
          "text": "The Same Seven Notes",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "minor-caged-the-same-seven-notes"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " proved the two keys share seven notes; this is the same fact one level up. The chords of a minor key were never a second thing to learn."
        }
      ]
    },
