    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "That is the whole of this lesson's work: the two forms that hold as full barres — the A form and "
        },
        {
          "text": "the E form",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "minor-caged-triad-e-form"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": ", the two chapter 2 handed you for free — are enough to play every chord in the key, twice over, without learning a new shape."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The exception is the honest one. "
        },
        {
          "text": "Bdim",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is "
        },
        {
          "text": "B D F",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — two minor thirds stacked, but the interval from "
        },
        {
          "text": "F",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " back up to "
        },
        {
          "text": "B",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is a tritone rather than a third, so it isn't the endlessly-repeating shape the four-note diminished-seventh chord is. There is no barre form of it, which is the practical, unglamorous reason "
        },
        {
          "text": "ii°",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " is the least-used chord in the key. "
        },
        {
          "text": "The Note That Hasn't Moved",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
